  - No tests, no lint config, no dependencies in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m.

  That split matters: the repo has two module conventions already, and a browser settings file can't use [38;5;153mrequire[39m. So the
  first fork is about how an environment actually gets chosen.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how does the running page know [3mwhich[0m environment it's in?

  - [1mRuntime detection from hostname[0m — settings ships all environments; the module picks one by looking at
    [38;5;153mwindow.location.hostname[39m. Buys: one artifact deploys everywhere, switching environments is editing a map in one
    file. Costs: all endpoint URLs are visible in the shipped source, and local/preview hostnames need entries. Cheap to
    change later.
  - [1mSingle active config, edited/swapped per deploy[0m — settings exports one flat config; you change the value (or swap
    the file) when you deploy elsewhere. Buys: dead simple, nothing conditional, only the live URL ships. Costs:
    switching means a file edit or a deploy-time copy, which is easy to get wrong by hand. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mBuild-time injection[0m — a build step substitutes values from env vars. Buys: the standard answer at scale,
    secrets/URLs stay out of source. Costs: introduces a bundler and a build to a repo that currently has neither, and
    [38;5;153mindex.html[39m would stop working as a plain file open. Expensive to undo.

  [1mMy recommendation: runtime detection from hostname.[0m It directly delivers "easier to change environments" — one map, no
  deploy choreography — and it keeps [38;5;153mindex.html[39m working as a plain static page with no toolchain added. Build-time
  injection is the right answer for a real app with secrets, but nothing here justifies adopting a bundler yet, and it's
  the one option that's costly to walk back.
